s old dependencies and sets new ones\n- `clearDependencies(cell)`: removes all outgoing edges from the cell\n\nHmm, let me make the API clean:\n\n```typescript\nclass DependencyGraph {\n  // ... internal maps\n  \n  // Get all cells that the given cell depends on\n  getDependencies(cell: string): Set<string>;\n  \n  // Get all cells that depend on the given cell\n  getDependents(cell: string): Set<string>;\n  \n  // Remove all dependencies for a cell (outgoing edges)\n  clearDependencies(cell: string): void;\n  \n  // Add dependencies for a cell (outgoing edges)\n  addDependencies(cell: string, deps: string[]): void;\n  \n  // Set dependencies: clear old, add new\n  setDependencies(cell: string, deps: string[]): void;\n  \n  // Check if adding dependencies for a cell would create a cycle\n  wouldCreateCycle(cell: string, deps: string[]): boolean;\n  \n  // Get all transitive dependents of a cell\n  getTransitiveDependents(cell: string): Set<string>;\n  \n  // Topologically sort a set of cells\n  topologicalSort(cells: string[]): string[];\n}\n```\n\nLet me code this properly.\n\nOK, I've planned enough. Let me write the actual code now.\n\nLet me write a comprehensive CONTRACT.md, then files:\n\n1. src/types.ts\n2. src/cell-reference.ts  \n3. src/formula-engine.ts (lexer, parser, evaluator, functions)\n4. src/dependency-graph.ts\n5. src/index.ts\n6. src/__tests__/spreadsheet.test.ts\n\nLet me write them all.\n\nOne more thing about the `:` token in lexing. The `:` is used only for ranges. When I encounter `:` in the formula string, it becomes a COLON token. The parser handles COLON when it appears after a CELL_REF in the `primary` production.\n\nActually, I realize there's a subtlety. In the grammar, `primary` is:\n\n```\nprimary → NUMBER | STRING | \"true\" | \"false\"\n        → CELL_REF (\":\" CELL_REF)?\n        → \"(\" expression \")\"\n```\n\nWhen the parser is in the `primary` function and sees a CELL_REF, it should check if the next token is COLON. If so, parse another CELL_REF and create a range node. Otherwise, just create a cell reference node.\n\nBut the COLON token is only produced when we see `:` in the formula. Since `:` is only used for ranges, this is unambiguous.\n\nLet me also think about how the AST represents ranges:\n\n```typescript\ninterface RangeNode {\n  kind: 'range';\n  start: string;  // \"A1\"\n  end: string;    // \"C3\"\n}\n```\n\nAnd cell references:\n\n```typescript\ninterface CellRefNode {\n  kind: 'cellRef';\n  ref: string;  // \"A1\"\n}\n```\n\nIn the evaluator:\n- CellRefNode: look up the cell's value\n- RangeNode: expand to all cells in the range, return array of values\n\nOK, I'm writing the code NOW.\n\nLet me write all the code files, then run the tests once.\n\nI think I can improve the evaluator's handling of numbers. When a cell reference evaluates to null (empty cell), I should treat it as 0 for arithmetic operations. But for direct evaluation (like `get()`), return null.\n\nActually, in the evaluator, when evaluating a cell reference node, it should return the cell's value (which could be null for empty cells). Let the arithmetic operators handle null by converting to 0.\n\n```typescript\ncase 'cellRef':\n  const val = this.get(ast.ref); // Could be null\n  return val ?? 0; // Treat null as 0 in formula evaluation\n```\n\nHmm, but what about strings? If a cell contains a string \"hello\" and I try to add it: `=A1+1` where A1=\"hello\". In JavaScript, \"hello\" + 1 = \"hello1\" (string concatenation). But in spreadsheet context, this should probably be an error or treat the string as 0.\n\nFor simplicity, I'll let JavaScript handle it. If someone writes `=A1+1` and A1 is \"hello\", the result would be \"hello1\". This is string concatenation, which might be unexpected but it's how the math works.\n\nActually wait, in the evaluator, when I do binary operations, I do:\n```typescript\ncase '+':\n  return left + right;\n```\n\nIf left is number and right is string, JavaScript will convert the number to string and concatenate. So `1 + \"hello\"` = `\"1hello\"`. That's probably not desired for a spreadsheet.\n\nLet me handle type coercion: if both operands are numbers, do arithmetic. If one is a string, convert to number (parseFloat or 0).\n\nActually, for the spec's purposes, cell values will mostly be numbers. Let me just throw a type error or handle gracefully. But for simplicity, I'll treat non-numeric values as 0 in arithmetic:\n\n```typescript\ncase '+': {\n  const l = typeof left === 'number' ? left : Number(left) || 0;\n  const r = typeof right === 'number' ? right : Number(right) || 0;\n  return l + r;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but this would convert boolean true to 1 and boolean false to 0. That might actually be desired. And strings that can't be parsed to numbers become 0.\n\nActually, let me just go simple:\n- If a value is already a number, use it\n- If it's null, use 0  \n- If it's boolean, convert to 1/0\n- If it's a string, try parseFloat; if NaN, use 0\n\nI'll create a helper `toNumber(value): number`:\n\n```typescript\nfunction toNumber(value: any): number {\n  if (value === null || value === undefined) return 0;\n  if (typeof value === 'number') return value;\n  if (typeof value === 'boolean') return value ? 1 : 0;\n  if (typeof value === 'string') {\n    const n = parseFloat(value);\n    return isNaN(n) ? 0 : n;\n  }\n  return 0;\n}\n```\n\nFor comparison operators, I need to handle both numeric and string comparisons. But comparisons in the spec are only used in IF conditions, which compare numbers. So I'll use toNumber for comparison operands too.\n\nOK, let me also think about the order of operations for comparison operators:\n\n```\ncomparison → term ((\">\" | \">=\" | \"<\" | \"<=\") term)*\nequality → comparison ((\"=\" | \"<>\") comparison)*\n```\n\nThis means `<` has higher precedence than `=` (equality). So `A1>B1=C1<D1` would be parsed as `(A1>B1) = (C1<D1)`. That seems reasonable.\n\nBut actually, in the grammar, `=` and `<>` are at the lowest precedence level (equality), and `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` are at the comparison level. So:\n\n```\nexpression → equality\nequality → comparison ((\"=\" | \"<>\") comparison)*\ncomparison → term ((\">\" | \">=\" | \"<\" | \"<=\") term)*\n```\n\nThis means `A1=B1>5` would parse as `A1 = (B1 > 5)`. The `B1>5` comparison evaluates to boolean, then `A1 = (boolean)` checks if A1 equals that boolean. That... might not be what users expect, but it's the standard precedence.\n\nIn practice, the most common usage is `A1>B1` or `A1=30` or `A1>=B1`, all simple comparisons. Let's not worry about chaining.\n\nOK, I need to write code now. Let me be efficient and write everything in large batches.\n\nLet me think about the file structure one more time. I'll try to keep it focused:\n\n1. `src/cell-reference.ts` - Cell reference and range operations\n2. `src/formula-engine.ts` - Lexer, parser, evaluator, built-in functions (including AST types and formula parsing/execution)\n3. `src/dependency-graph.ts` - Dependency graph management\n4. `src/index.ts` - Spreadsheet class (main export)\n5. `tests/spreadsheet.test.ts` - Tests\n6. `CONTRACT.md` - Contract\n\nWait, I said \"all code in src/\". So tests should be in src/. Let me put tests in `src/__tests__/`.\n\nHmm, but `tests/` is excluded from the tsconfig, while `src/` is not. If I put tests in `src/__tests__/`, the TypeScript compiler might try to compile test files with import paths that don't work. But since we're using vitest with tsx/ts-node, it should be fine. And the `exclude` in tsconfig only matters for `tsc --build`, not for vitest.\n\nActually, looking at the tsconfig:\n```json\n\"include\": [\"src/**/*\"],\n\"exclude\": [\"node_modules\", \"dist\", \"tests\"]\n```\n\nIf I put tests in `src/__tests__/`, they'll be included in the tsc compilation (since `include` is `src/**/*` and `exclude` doesn't exclude `src/__tests__`). But that's fine because vitest handles the compilation.\n\nLet me put tests in `src/__tests__/spreadsheet.test.ts`.\n\nOK let me write all the code now. I'll be thorough and comprehensive.\n\nActually, I just realized: I should run the tests and build to make sure everything works. Let me code first, then test.\n\nLet me think about one more thing: how to handle `=` as both the formula prefix and the comparison operator.\n\nIn the formula `=A1=5`:\n- Strip the initial `=`: `A1=5`\n- Tokenize: CELL_REF(A1), EQ_COMP, NUMBER(5)\n- Parse: binary comparison `=` with left=A1, right=5\n- Evaluate: check if A1's value equals 5\n\nIn the formula `=IF(A1=5, \"yes\", \"no\")`:\n- Strip the initial `=`: `IF(A1=5, \"yes\", \"no\")`\n\nIn the tokenizer, when we see `=`, we need to know it's a comparison operator (since the leading `=` was already stripped). So `=` inside the expression is always EQ_COMP.\n\nBut wait, `=` is used for equality comparison. But `==` is not used (the spec shows `=` as the comparison operator). And `===` is also not used.\n\nSo in the tokenizer:\n- When we see `=`, emit EQ token\n- No need to look ahead for `==` or `===`\n\nBut wait, could `=` be confused with range separator? No, ranges use `:`, not `=`.\n\nCould `=` be confused with assignment? In spreadsheet formulas, there's no assignment operator inside expressions. So `=` is always comparison.\n\nGood, so the tokenizer is simple: `=` → EQ token.\n\nBut I need to be careful about one thing: `>=` starts with `>`, not `=`. And `<=` starts with `<`, not `=`. And `<>` starts with `<`, not `=`. So `=` is unambiguous as the equality comparison.\n\nAlright, let me actually write the code now!\n\nLet me organize my thoughts into code:\n\n## src/cell-reference.ts\n\n```typescript\nexport interface CellRef {\n  col: string;\n  row: number;\n}\n\nexport function parseCellRef(ref: string): CellRef {\n  const match = ref.match(/^([A-Za-z]+)(\\d+)$/);\n  if (!match) throw new Error(`Invalid cell reference: ${ref}`);\n  return { col: match[1].toUpperCase(), row: parseInt(match[2], 10) };\n}\n\nexport function cellRefToString(ref: CellRef): string {\n  return `${ref.col}${ref.row}`;\n}\n\nexport function normalizeCellRef(ref: string): string {\n  return cellRefToString(parseCellRef(ref));\n}\n\nexport function colToNumber(col: string): number {\n  let result = 0;\n  for (const char of col) {\n    result = result * 26 + (char.charCodeAt(0) - 64); // 'A' = 65\n  }\n  return result - 1; // 0-indexed\n}\n\nexport function numberToCol(num: number): string {\n  let result = '';\n  num++; // 1-indexed\n  while (num > 0) {\n    num--;\n    result = String.fromCharCode((num % 26) + 65) + result;\n    num = Math.floor(num / 26);\n  }\n  return result;\n}\n\nexport function expandRange(start: string, end: string): string[] {\n  const startRef = parseCellRef(start);\n  const endRef = parseCellRef(end);\n  \n  const startCol = colToNumber(startRef.col);\n  const endCol = colToNumber(endRef.col);\n  const startRow = startRef.row;\n  const endRow = endRef.row;\n  \n  const minCol = Math.min(startCol, endCol);\n  const maxCol = Math.max(startCol, endCol);\n  const minRow = Math.min(startRow, endRow);\n  const maxRow = Math.max(startRow, endRow);\n  \n  const cells: string[] = [];\n  for (let c = minCol; c <= maxCol; c++) {\n    for (let r = minRow; r <= maxRow; r++) {\n      cells.push(`${numberToCol(c)}${r}`);\n    }\n  }\n  return cells;\n}\n\nexport function isValidCellRef(ref: string): boolean {\n  return /^[A-Za-z]+\\d+$/.test(ref);\n}\n```\n\n## src/formula-engine.ts\n\nThis will be the big one. Let me structure it carefully.\n\n```typescript\n// ---- AST Types ----\nexport type Expr = \n  | { kind: 'number'; value: number }\n  | { kind: 'string'; value: string }\n  | { kind: 'boolean'; value: boolean }\n  | { kind: 'cellRef'; ref: string }\n  | { kind: 'range'; start: string; end: string }\n  | { kind: 'binary'; op: string; left: Expr; right: Expr }\n  | { kind: 'unary'; op: string; operand: Expr }\n  | { kind: 'function'; name: string; args: Expr[] };\n\n// ---- Tokenizer ----\n// ... \n\n// ---- Parser ----\n// ...\n\n// ---- Evaluator ----\n// ...\n\n// ---- Extract References ----\nexport function extractCellReferences(ast: Expr): string[] {\n  const refs = new Set<string>();\n  function walk(node: Expr) {\n    switch (node.kind) {\n      case 'cellRef': refs.add(node.ref); break;\n      case 'range':\n        // Expand range to individual cells\n        const cells = expandRange(node.start, node.end);\n        cells.forEach(c => refs.add(c));\n        break;\n      case 'binary':\n        walk(node.left); walk(node.right);\n        break;\n      case 'unary':\n        walk(node.operand);\n        break;\n      case 'function':\n        node.args.forEach(walk);\n        break;\n    }\n  }\n  walk(ast);\n  return [...refs];\n}\n```\n\nLet me write the evaluator:\n\n```typescript\nexport function evaluateAST(ast: Expr, getCellValue: (ref: string) => any): any {\n  switch (ast.kind) {\n    case 'number': return ast.value;\n    case 'string': return ast.value;\n    case 'boolean': return ast.value;\n    case 'cellRef': {\n      const val = getCellValue(ast.ref);\n      return val === null || val === undefined ? 0 : val;\n    }\n    case 'range': {\n      const cells = expandRange(ast.start, ast.end);\n      return cells.map(c => {\n        const val = getCellValue(c);\n        return val === null || val === undefined ? 0 : val;\n      });\n    }\n    case 'binary': {\n      const left = evaluateAST(ast.left, getCellValue);\n      const right = evaluateAST(ast.right, getCellValue);\n      switch (ast.op) {\n        case '+': return toNumber(left) + toNumber(right);\n        case '-': return toNumber(left) - toNumber(right);\n        case '*': return toNumber(left) * toNumber(right);\n        case '/': return toNumber(left) / toNumber(right);\n        case '>': return toNumber(left) > toNumber(right);\n        case '<': return toNumber(left) < toNumber(right);\n        case '>=': return toNumber(left) >= toNumber(right);\n        case '<=': return toNumber(left) <= toNumber(right);\n        case '=': return toNumber(left) === toNumber(right);\n        case '<>': return toNumber(left) !== toNumber(right);\n        default: throw new Error(`Unknown binary operator: ${ast.op}`);\n      }\n    }\n    case 'unary': {\n      const operand = evaluateAST(ast.operand, getCellValue);\n      if (ast.op === '-') return -toNumber(operand);\n      throw new Error(`Unknown unary operator: ${ast.op}`);\n    }\n    case 'function': {\n      const args = ast.args.map(a => evaluateAST(a, getCellValue));\n      return callFunction(ast.name, args, getCellValue);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction toNumber(value: any): number {\n  if (value === null || value === undefined) return 0;\n  if (typeof value === 'number') return value;\n  if (typeof value === 'boolean') return value ? 1 : 0;\n  if (typeof value === 'string') {\n    const n = parseFloat(value);\n    return isNaN(n) ? 0 : n;\n  }\n  return 0;\n}\n\nfunction callFunction(name: string, args: any[], getCellValue: (ref: string) => any): any {\n  switch (name.toUpperCase()) {\n    case 'SUM': {\n      const values = expandFunctionArgs(args);\n      return values.reduce((sum, v) => sum + toNumber(v), 0);\n    }\n    case 'AVERAGE': {\n      const values = expandFunctionArgs(args);\n      const nums = values.filter(v => typeof v === 'number');\n      if (nums.length === 0) return 0;\n      return nums.reduce((sum, v) => sum + v, 0) / nums.length;\n    }\n    case 'MIN': {\n      const values = expandFunctionArgs(args);\n      const nums = values.filter(v => typeof v === 'number');\n      if (nums.length === 0) return 0;\n      return Math.min(...nums);\n    }\n    case 'MAX': {\n      const values = expandFunctionArgs(args);\n      const nums = values.filter(v => typeof v === 'number');\n      if (nums.length === 0) return 0;\n      return Math.max(...nums);\n    }\n    case 'COUNT': {\n      const values = expandFunctionArgs(args);\n      return values.filter(v => typeof v === 'number').length;\n    }\n    case 'IF': {\n      if (args.length !== 3) throw new Error('IF requires 3 arguments');\n      const condition = args[0];\n      // Treat condition as boolean: truthy = use trueValue, falsy = use falseValue\n      return condition ? evaluateAST(args[1] as Expr, getCellValue) : evaluateAST(args[2] as Expr, getCellValue);\n    }\n    default: throw new Error(`Unknown function: ${name}`);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait, the IF function is tricky. The arguments to IF are expressions, but when we call `evaluateAST` on the function node, we've already evaluated all the arguments. So `args` contains the evaluated values, not the AST nodes.\n\nBut for IF, we only want to evaluate the branch that's selected. If the condition is true, we should only evaluate the trueValue, not the falseValue.\n\nThis means I need to change how function arguments are evaluated: for IF, evaluate lazily.\n\nOption 1: Pass AST nodes directly to the function handler.\nOption 2: Have a special evaluation for IF.\n\nLet me go with Option 1: the function handler receives the AST nodes and evaluates them lazily.\n\nActually, that changes the evaluator interface. Let me think about this differently.\n\nWhen evaluating a function node, I could:\n1. Evaluate all arguments eagerly (current approach)\n2. Pass them to the function handler\n\nFor IF, eager evaluation doesn't work because we'd evaluate both branches before checking the condition.\n\nAlternative approach: have the function handler accept raw (unevaluated) arguments and evaluate them lazily.\n\nLet me redesign:\n\n```typescript\ncase 'function': {\n  return callFunction(ast.name, ast.args, getCellValue);\n}\n\nfunction callFunction(name: string, args: Expr[], getCellValue: (ref: string) => any): any {\n  switch (name.toUpperCase()) {\n    case 'SUM': {\n      const values = args.flatMap(a => expandArg(a, getCellValue));\n      return values.reduce((sum, v) => sum + toNumber(v), 0);\n    }\n    case 'IF': {\n      if (args.length !== 3) throw new Error('IF requires 3 arguments');\n      const condition = evaluateAST(args[0], getCellValue);\n      return condition ? evaluateAST(args[1], getCellValue) : evaluateAST(args[2], getCellValue);\n    }\n    // ...\n  }\n}\n\nfunction expandArg(arg: Expr, getCellValue: (ref: string) => any): any[] {\n  if (arg.kind === 'range') {\n    const cells = expandRange(arg.start, arg.end);\n    return cells.map(c => getCellValue(c));\n  }\n  return [evaluateAST(arg, getCellValue)];\n}\n```\n\nThis approach:\n- Function handlers receive AST nodes (Expr[]), not evaluated values\n- They evaluate lazily using evaluateAST\n- For SUM/AVERAGE/etc., args that are ranges are expanded to their cell values\n- For IF, only the selected branch is evaluated\n\nLet me refine this. The function handler receives args and a getCellValue callback.\n\nFor SUM(A1:A5, B1:B5):\n- args[0] is a range node (A1:A5)\n- args[1] is a range node (B1:B5)\n- SUM handler expands each arg: gets all cell values in each range\n- Flattens and sums\n\nFor SUM(A1, A2):\n- args[0] is a cell ref node (A1)\n- args[1] is a cell ref node (A2)\n- Each evaluates to a single value\n\nFor SUM(A1:A5, B1):\n- args[0] is a range (A1:A5)\n- args[1] is a cell ref (B1)\n- Both evaluated and summed\n\nLet me write the expandArg function:\n\n```typescript\nfunction expandArg(arg: Expr, getCellValue: (ref: string) => any): any[] {\n  if (arg.kind === 'range') {\n    const cells = expandRange(arg.start, arg.end);\n    return cells.map(c => {\n      const val = getCellValue(c);\n      return val === null || val === undefined ? 0 : val;\n    });\n  }\n  const val = evaluateAST(arg, getCellValue);\n  return [val === null || val === undefined ? 0 : val];\n}\n```\n\nActually, for non-range args (numbers, strings, booleans, cell refs, expressions), we evaluate them as-is. For ranges, we expand and look up each cell.\n\nHere's the refined implementation:\n\n```typescript\nfunction callFunction(name: string, arg